<PageTitle>About — Blazor Atoms (Server)</PageTitle>

<h1>About this demo — Blazor Server</h1>

<h2>The three samples</h2>
<p>
    Every playground in this repository is written once, in <code>Demos.Shared</code>, and hosted by
    three sibling apps — one per Blazor hosting model. Running the same components three ways is the
    point: it is how a component that quietly depends on one model gets caught.
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>BlazorWebAppSvrDemo</strong> — Blazor Server. <em>You are here.</em></li>
    <li><strong>BlazorWebAppAutoDemo</strong> — Blazor Auto, server-interactive first, WebAssembly once cached.</li>
    <li><strong>BlazorWebAppWasmDemo</strong> — standalone WebAssembly, no server host at all.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What this one demonstrates</h2>
<p>
    Every page here is marked <code>@@rendermode InteractiveServer</code>: component code runs on the
    server and UI diffs reach the browser over a SignalR circuit. No WebAssembly runtime is involved.
    This is the sample that proves the libraries need nothing browser-side — anything assuming it
    runs in the browser fails here and nowhere else. Self-loading JavaScript modules still work
    normally, but each interop call is a network round-trip rather than in-process, so this is also
    where interop chattiness shows.
</p>
<p>
    Shape: one ASP.NET Core project, no <code>.Client</code> companion. It raises SignalR's inbound
    message limit to 5&nbsp;MB, because a few playgrounds push large payloads over the circuit —
    pasting an image into the QR decoder, for instance, which is a base64 round-trip here but a local
    canvas read under WebAssembly. A <a href="RouteInfo">route dump</a> lists every
    <code>@@page</code> template discovered by reflection.
</p>

